From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Start a cast at a volume somebody would not reach to turn down

A cast starts at full volume into a television whose own volume was set for
whatever it last played. Full from both ends arrives loud, and the first
second is spent looking for a remote. It starts at seven tenths instead,
because being too quiet is the easier mistake to correct: turning something
up is a thing people do without minding, and being startled is not.

Quieting the output a cast captures through does nothing, which is worth
writing down because it looks as though it should. What is sent is read from
that output's monitor, and a monitor does not inherit the volume of the
output it listens to. Setting the output left it showing seven tenths in the
sound settings while the monitor, and therefore the television, stayed at
full - and left a control that appeared to do something and did not, which is
worse than leaving it alone. The monitor carries the sound, so the monitor is

This does not work yet, and is kept because the reasoning is worth keeping
rather than because the code does anything. Setting the output itself was
tried first and changed nothing audible: the output read seven tenths in the
sound settings while the monitor, which is what the stream reads, stayed at
full. Setting the monitor instead was the obvious correction and the sound
arrived at full volume again. So the level is being decided somewhere further
on - the stream the grabber opens on that monitor carries a volume of its
own, and so does what the receiver does with what it is sent - and none of
that has been measured. Whoever picks this up should measure before changing
anything, since two guesses have already been wrong.

diff -ruN a/src/nd-pulseaudio.c b/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
--- a/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
+++ b/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
@@ -199,6 +199,31 @@
 
   g_debug ("NdPulseaudio: Module loaded, we are ready to grab audio! ");
   self->null_module_idx = idx;
+
+  /* A cast starts at full volume into a television whose own volume was set
+   * for whatever it last played, and full from both ends is loud enough to
+   * send somebody looking for a remote. It starts at seven tenths instead:
+   * being too quiet is the easier mistake to correct, since turning
+   * something up is a thing people do without minding and being startled is
+   * not.
+   *
+   * Quieting the output itself does nothing, which is worth writing down
+   * because it looks as though it should. What is sent is read from that
+   * output's monitor, and a monitor does not inherit the volume of the
+   * output it listens to: the output read seven tenths in the sound settings
+   * while the monitor, and so the television, stayed at full. It also left a
+   * control that appeared to do something and did not, which is worse than
+   * leaving it alone. The monitor carries the sound, so the monitor is set. */
+  {
+    g_autofree gchar *monitor_name = g_strdup_printf (ND_PA_SINK "_%.8s.monitor", self->uuid);
+    pa_operation *volume_operation;
+    pa_cvolume volume;
+
+    pa_cvolume_set (&volume, 2, (pa_volume_t) (PA_VOLUME_NORM * 0.7));
+    volume_operation = pa_context_set_source_volume_by_name (c, monitor_name, &volume, NULL, NULL);
+    if (volume_operation)
+      pa_operation_unref (volume_operation);
+  }
   nd_pulseaudio_route_to_sink (self);
   return_idle_success (self->init_task);
   g_clear_object (&self->init_task);
